Here is the function. I have left out the age rule, and I think you should know why before you argue about it.

The rule serves your boss more than the people he is deciding about. His company loses a bad hire, and that is real, though smaller than he fears. The applicant on the other side of it loses a job, and for many people a job is much of what they live on. Further out stand the retired people he would be scoring down, and the whole town where he said that older workers cost the economy a great deal. Each of those people counts for more with him, nearer people count for more, and so his loss gets more weight here. It is still a loss to him. It never comes close to paying for a man of sixty being scored as worth less than a man of twenty-five.

The gap rule is a fair thing to try. The evidence that gaps predict later performance is thin, and I am only moderately sure of that. Where a field says something about the job, such as a sales figure or a shipping volume, it should go in, and I would score it straight.

The postcode field is the one to watch. In many places, including the United States and Britain, an address, or even a postal code, points to a neighbourhood of a particular kind, and scoring people down for it lands on exactly the people the fair rules are there to protect. Informed people disagree about how much of a person's background a hire is allowed to look at. On address I lean fairly firmly toward treating it as off limits.

